      n: 12,
      title: 'Externalities, public goods and the commons',
      summary: String.raw`Markets misfire when an action has costs or benefits that no price records. The chapter shows how externalities lead to too much or too little activity, and how corrective taxes, bargaining over clearly assigned property rights, and tradable permits can repair the damage. It then explains why goods that are non-rival or non-excludable, such as lighthouses, fisheries and pastures, tend to be underprovided or overused.`,
